{"causes": ["The power vacuum following the death of Philip II of Macedon allowed Paeonian kings like Patraus to assert or maintain a degree of independence.", "The strategic geographical position of Paeonia between Macedon and Thracian territories necessitated a strong, independent ruler to manage regional alliances and threats.", "The established tradition of Paeonian kings striking their own coinage provided Patraus with a means to project royal authority and stabilize the local economy."], "effects": ["Patraus' continued issuance of silver coinage helped maintain a distinct Paeonian economic identity amidst the growing influence of Macedonian currency.", "His reign preserved Paeonian political autonomy for a critical period, delaying its full annexation into the Macedonian sphere of influence.", "The numismatic evidence from Patraus' era serves as a primary source for understanding Paeonian history and royal succession during the early Hellenistic period."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "patraus", "sourceName": "Patraus", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "wars-of-the-diadochi", "targetName": "Wars of the Diadochi", "context": "Patraus' reign in Paeonia from around 335 to 314 BCE coincided with the turbulent Wars of the Diadochi, which reshaped the Hellenistic world."}, {"sourceSlug": "patraus", "sourceName": "Patraus", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "paeonian-silver-coinage", "targetName": "Paeonian Silver Coinage", "context": "Patraus issued distinctive silver tetradrachms and drachms, which are the primary historical evidence of his rule and Paeonian sovereignty."}, {"sourceSlug": "paeonian-kingdom", "sourceName": "Paeonian Kingdom", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "patraus", "targetName": "Patraus", "context": "The Paeonian Kingdom was the ancient polity over which Patraus ruled as king from approximately 335 to 314 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "alexander-iii-of-macedon", "sourceName": "Alexander III of Macedon", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "patraus", "targetName": "Patraus", "context": "The expansionist policies of Alexander the Great and his predecessors significantly influenced the political landscape Patraus navigated as king of Paeonia."}, {"sourceSlug": "paeonia", "sourceName": "Paeonia", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ancient-greece", "targetName": "Ancient Greece", "context": "The kingdom of Paeonia, where Patraus ruled, was geographically located north of ancient Macedon, within the broader sphere of Ancient Greece."}], "places": [{"name": "Paeonia, Ancient Greece", "role": "Kingdom's Seat"}, {"name": "Macedon, Ancient Greece", "role": "Dominant Neighbor"}, {"name": "Balkan Peninsula, Europe", "role": "Geographic Region"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Patraus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "patraus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q14472288", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patraus"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 5, "significanceNarrative": "Patraus' reign maintained a fragile period of Paeonian autonomy through the turbulent Wars of the Diadochi, evidenced primarily by his distinct coinage. This preserved a separate cultural and political identity before the eventual full Macedonian annexation.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-25T17:11:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45661410130", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Patraus was king of the ancient kingdom of Paeonia from around 335 until his death in 315 BC. Patraus' relationship with the previous Paeonian king, Lyppeius, is unknown, as are the circumstances surrounding his ascension. Aside from the silver coinage struck in his name, we know nothing for certain about the events of Patraus' reign.", "newValue": "Patraus reigned as king of the ancient kingdom of Paeonia from approximately 335 BCE until his death in 314 BCE, presiding over a period of continued semi-autonomy amidst the expanding Macedonian hegemony. Positioned north of Macedon, Paeonia, under Patraus, navigated a complex geopolitical landscape shaped by the successors of Alexander the Great. His rule is primarily attested through the distinctive silver coinage issued in his name, providing tangible evidence of a functioning royal administration capable of minting currency, a crucial marker of sovereignty in the Hellenistic world.

While the precise circumstances of Patraus' ascension and his familial ties to his predecessor, Lyppeius, remain obscure, his reign occurred within the tumultuous Wars of the Diadochi. Patraus maintained Paeonian independence, at least nominally, despite the overwhelming power of the Macedonian Empire. His silver tetradrachms and drachms, often depicting Apollo or Heracles on the obverse and a horse or horseman on the reverse, served not only as economic instruments but also as powerful symbols of Paeonian identity and royal authority, circulating widely in the region and providing the main historical window into his rule.

Patraus' ability to sustain the Paeonian kingdom as a distinct entity, even as a client state, prevented its immediate absorption into the nascent Hellenistic empires. His reign thus represents a critical bridge period, preserving a degree of Paeonian cultural and political continuity before its eventual complete integration into Macedon. The survival of his coinage suggests a stable, albeit perhaps limited, economy and a recognized royal lineage, influencing regional trade and affirming Paeonian presence on the periphery of the Hellenistic world."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-25T17:11:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45661410130", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "339 BCE"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "339 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The power vacuum following the death of Philip II of Macedon allowed Paeonian kings like Patraus to assert or maintain a degree of independence.", "The strategic geographical position of Paeonia between Macedon and Thracian territories necessitated a strong, independent ruler to manage regional alliances and threats.", "The established tradition of Paeonian kings striking their own coinage provided Patraus with a means to project royal authority and stabilize the local economy."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-25T17:11:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45661410130", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "314 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "314 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Patraus' continued issuance of silver coinage helped maintain a distinct Paeonian economic identity amidst the growing influence of Macedonian c